M3-c208 back: Q^T Q = I and Q Q^T = I, where I is the identity matrix (Definition 1.2a).
M3-c101 back: A square real matrix Q whose columns are orthonormal, that is, Q^T Q = I (Definition 1.1).
M3-c205 back: Yes, assuming F1 of Lesson 4 (Fact 1.2). Applying F1 to M = Q^T and N = Q shows that Q^T is invertible with inverse Q, so Q Q^T = I as well.
